 #:seon.task{:name "a-branch-agent-assigned-after-its-fork-never-gets-a-model-turn"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.turn/next-agent-work`'s :open arm (CX-2) reads the held assignment with `seon.task/agent-task` on the agent's own database value, but assignments live at the task home (`seon.task/home`), where `seon.task/assign!` writes them and every other reader (`seon.context/current-state`, `seon.render.ns`, `seon.cluster.status`, the step) looks. An agent whose branch forked before root assigned it never sees its assignment there, so its wakes stay unanswered and no model turn ever opens (live-agent run 3, default 0de932400, 2026-10-08): agent live-flash-3b on branch :agent-6ea18cf66f12, assigned on main after the fork, provider enabled on the branch, root's message delivered to the branch; (seon.task/agent-task branch-db \"live-flash-3b\") is nil, on main it is \"504845114355\", and (seon.turn/next-agent-work branch-db {:seon.agent/id \"live-flash-3b\"}) is nil. The run went ahead only by creating the agent row on main with its branch named, assigning it, and then forking the branch, so the branch inherited the assignment. `seon.turn-work-test/an-unassigned-agent-with-a-wake-gets-no-model-turn` keeps its agent on the task home, so it cannot see this. Done when an agent on its own branch, assigned at the home after its branch forked, opens a model turn on its next wake (read the assignment from `(or (some-> (seon.task/home db) seon.db/db) db)`, as `seon.context/current-state` does)."
             :must-read #{seon.turn/next-agent-work seon.task/agent-task seon.task/home seon.context/current-state}}
